API::Docker has resource classes for images, containers, networks, volumes,
system and exec, but nothing for the distribution endpoint:

  GET /distribution/{name}/json

The daemon uses it to query a *registry* for an image manifest without
pulling - 200 with the descriptor when the reference exists there, 404 when
it does not. Registry auth goes through the same X-Registry-Auth header as
push.

Requested by ../p5-dist-zilla-plugin-docker-api (its karr #6): the plugin
has a fail_if_tag_exists option whose remote_tag_exists is a stub returning
a constant "no", because there is no way to ask. It is documented as not
implemented until this exists.

Not urgent - the consumer ships with the option documented as a no-op.

2026-08-27 05:20 Implemented as two methods on API::Docker::API::Distribution, and the
"Scaffolding only" paragraph is gone.

  $docker->distribution->inspect($ref, auth => $auth, response => \%res)
  $docker->distribution->exists($ref, auth => $auth)

The 404 question, resolved as a split rather than a single choice:

  inspect  is the endpoint and croaks on any status >= 400, 404 included, like
           every other method here. response => \%res is filled before the
           croak, so an eval-ing caller reaches the status.
  exists   is the question and answers it: 1, 0, or a croak -- callable
           without eval, because everything it returns is an answer from the
           registry and everything else is loud.

Why exists cannot simply read 404 as "no": measured, rootless Podman 5.4.2
(API 1.41) serves NO route for this endpoint at all --
  GET /v1.41/distribution/nginx:latest/json
  -> 404 {"cause":"","message":"Path ... is not supported","response":0}
-- and the same for a bare name and a percent-escaped reference. A naive
"404 means no" would answer no for every image on that engine, i.e. exactly
the constant "no" of the consumer stub, reintroduced one layer down where it
looks like an answer. exists tells the engine talking about itself apart from
the registry talking about the reference ("is not supported" / "page not
found") and croaks on the former. An unrecognised wording degrades to false,
which is what a plain 404-means-no would have done anyway.

Live-verified against the Podman socket: exists("nginx:latest") croaks with
"cannot ask this engine", it does not return 0.

Registry auth is sent only when auth was given (anonymous lookup of a public
image sends no header), unlike images->push which always sends one.

